# Code Documentation Standards

## The "Why, Not How" Principle
Code explains how something works. Documentation explains:
- Why a decision was made.
- What contract a public API guarantees.
- What trade-offs were accepted.

Comments that restate what the code already clearly shows are noise and MUST be removed.

```java
// BAD: What does this comment add?
// Increment counter by 1
counter++;

// GOOD: Explains non-obvious reasoning
// Use AtomicInteger here even though this is single-threaded;
// this service will be made multi-tenant in Q3 (see ADR-012).
counter.incrementAndGet();
```

## Documentation as Code
- Documentation MUST live in the same repository as the code.
- Documentation updates MUST be part of the same commit/PR as the code change.
- Documentation review is a MANDATORY part of the code review checklist.
- Outdated documentation is a defect — treat it as one.

## README Standard
Every repository MUST have a `README.md` containing:
- Project name and one-sentence description
- Prerequisites — runtime versions, required tools
- Setup / Installation — numbered steps, runnable as-is
- Running Locally — how to start the service
- Running Tests — how to execute the test suite
- Environment Variables — table of all env vars, their purpose, and whether required
- Architecture Overview — brief description or link to ADR index
- Contributing — PR process, branch conventions
- License

## Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)
Any architectural decision with significant consequences MUST be recorded using the ADR format:
```markdown
# ADR-{number}: {Title}

**Status:** Proposed | Accepted | Deprecated | Superseded by ADR-{N}
**Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
**Deciders:** {list of people involved}

## Context
What is the issue that motivates this decision?

## Decision
What is the change being proposed or accepted?

## Consequences
What becomes easier or harder as a result of this decision?
```

ADRs are immutable once accepted — never edit; supersede with a new ADR.

## Language-Specific Documentation Formats
- **Java:** Javadoc (`/** */`) — `@param`, `@return`, `@throws` mandatory for public APIs
- **Python:** Google-style docstrings — `Args`, `Returns`, `Raises` sections
- **TypeScript:** TSDoc (`/** */`) — `@param`, `@returns`, `@throws`
- **Go:** godoc comments (`//`) — must start with identifier name

## Public API Documentation Obligation
All public APIs MUST document:
- Parameters (with constraints)
- Return values (for both success and failure)
- Raised errors (with trigger conditions)
- Side effects
- Copy-pasteable examples

## Anti-Duplication Rules
- NO paraphrasing external tool documentation — link instead of copy-pasting
- Single source of truth for all documentation
- Do NOT document what the code signature already makes obvious

## Gold Standard Examples
- See `examples/` directory for documentation patterns in each language

## What NOT to Do
- Never leave outdated documentation in place (delete immediately)
- Never document what the signature already expresses
- Never commit commented-out code (use git history instead)
- Never copy-paste external documentation (link instead)
